# THE `**/` ON vendor IS LOAD-BEARING. Do not simplify it to `vendor`.
#
# This image never booted. Every container died before running a line of app
# code:
#
#     Fatal error: Failed opening required
#     '/app/vendor/composer/../tina4stack/tina4php/Tina4/Constants.php'
#
# The culprit is `example/vendor`, not the repo's own vendor. The example app is
# a normal Tina4 PHP app, so it vendors tina4php as a Packagist DEPENDENCY, and
# its generated autoloader resolves the eager `files` map to
# $vendorDir/tina4stack/tina4php/Tina4/Constants.php -- a path that does not
# exist even inside example/vendor (that install is partial), and one THIS repo
# can never produce: here Tina4 IS the project and the real file is
# Tina4/Bootstrap/Constants.php.
#
# The Dockerfile's LAST instruction copies the demo app over the app root:
#
#     COPY --from=composer-stage /build/example/ /app/
#
# so example/vendor lands on /app/vendor and replaces the correct autoloader
# composer had just generated, at the very end of the build.
#
# A bare `vendor` pattern does NOT prevent this. Docker matches .dockerignore
# patterns from the CONTEXT ROOT, so `vendor` means `./vendor` only and
# `example/vendor` sails straight through. `**/vendor` catches both.
#
# An earlier attempt blamed the repo's own 13 MB vendor/ and tried to patch the
# symptom by copying Tina4/ into the vendored path. That could never work: the
# stale map names several such paths and describes a different project layout.
**/vendor
**/composer.phar

.git
.github
.claude
.idea
node_modules

tests
benchmarks
coverage

logs
*.log
.env
.env.local
.DS_Store
